t the coefficients on the boundary are positive, so the appearance of internal zero coefficients will contradict the log-concavity property (25).\n\n###### Theorem 6.4. Image\n\nConjecture 6.3 holds for the Markov-Fibonacci polynomials.\n\n###### Proof.\n\nWe need to check the log-concavity condition in all three principal directions. Using (16), in the horizontal direction we have\n\n | {A}_{i,j}^{2}={\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n-j}{n+1-i-j}\\right)}^{2}{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j}{j}\\right)}^{2}=\\left(\\frac{{(n-j)!}^{2}}{{(n+1-i-j)!}^{2}{(i-1)!}^{2}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{(i+j)!}^{2}}{{i!}^{2}{j!}^{2}}\\right),  | \n | {A}_{i-1,j}{A}_{i+1,j}  | =\\left({\\displaystyle \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n-j}{n+2-i-j}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n-j}{n-i-j}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j-1}{j}}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j+1}{j}}\\right)  | \n\nwith both of the final fractions being greater than 1 (the first requires that i\\ge 1 but the vertical line for i=0 consists of only a single point, so can be ignored).\n\nIn the diagonal case the second binomials in {A}_{i-1,j+1},{A}_{i,j},{A}_{i+1,j-1} are respectively \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j}{j+1}\\right),\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j}{j}\\right),\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{i+j}{j-1}\\right), which are consecutive binomial coefficients satisfying the log-concavity property. As a result we have\n\n | \\frac{{A}_{i,j}^{2}}{{A}_{i-1,j+1}{A}_{i+1,j-1}}\\ge \\left(\\frac{{(n-j)!}^{2}}{(n-j+1)!(n-j-1)!}\\right)\\left(\\frac{(n-i-j)!(n+2-i-j)!}{{(n+1-i-j)!}^{2}}\\right)  | \n | =\\left(\\frac{n-j}{n-j+1}\\right)\\left(\\frac{n+2-i-j}{n+1-i-j}\\right)=\\frac{(n-j)(n+1-i-j)+(n-j)}{(n-j)(n+1-i-j)+(n+1-i-j)},  | \n\nwhich is greater than or equal to 1 as required. ∎\n\nThe Conjecture 6.3 holds along the boundaries of the polygon, since these coefficients are precisely binomial coefficients, which are known to be log-concave. We will show now that this holds also on the 2nd horizontal line and upper diagonal of the Newton polygon.\n\n###### Lemma 6.5. Image\n\nSequences of the form\n\n | {x}_{k}=A\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)+B\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right),k=0,1,\\mathrm{\\dots },c  | \n\nfor positive A,B,c are always log-concave.\n\n###### Proof.\n\nWe have\n\n | {x}_{k}^{2}={A}^{2}{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)}^{2}+2AB\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)+{B}^{2}{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)}^{2},  | \n | \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{k-1}{x}_{k+1}={A}^{2}\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k+1}\\right)+AB\\left[\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)+\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-2}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k+1}\\right)\\right]\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill +{B}^{2}\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-2}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right).\\end{array}  | \n\nIt is clear that the coefficients of {A}^{2} and {B}^{2} are greater in the case of {x}_{k}^{2} due to the log-concavity of the binomial coefficients. So if we can show that\n\n | \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)\\ge \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-2}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k+1}\\right),  | \n\nthen the result will follow. Since\n\n | \\frac{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-1}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k}\\right)}{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k-2}\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{c}{k+1}\\right)}=\\frac{k+1}{k-1}\\frac{c-k+2}{c-k}>1,  | \n\nthis completes the proof. ∎\n\n###### Theorem 6.6. Image\n\nThe coefficients of Markov polynomial {M}_{\\rho } that appear on the lines with j=1 and i+j=a+b-2 of its Newton polygon are strictly log-concave.\n\n###### Proof.\n\nThe proof follows from Theorem 4.2 and the previous lemma. ∎\n\nAs for the 3rd diagonal we are only able to prove the log-concavity in the case where \\rho \\le 3/5.\n\n###### Theorem 6.7. Image\n\nThe coefficients of Markov polynomials {M}_{\\rho } that appear on the line with i+j=a+b-3 of its Newton polygon are log-concave for \\frac{a}{b}\\le \\frac{3}{5}.\n\n###### Proof.\n\nWe will use the following result quoted by Stanley [31] (but which dates back to Newton [25]).\n\n###### Theorem 6.8 (Stanley [31]). Image\n\nLet p(X)={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n}{\\alpha }_{k}{X}^{k}. If the polynomial p(X) has only real zeros, then the sequence {\\alpha }_{k} is log-concave.\n\nLet {\\alpha }_{k} be the coefficients on the diagonal i+j-3 stated in Theorem 4.2:\n\n | {\\alpha }_{k}=E\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{a+b-3}{k}\\right)+F\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{a+b-4}{k-1}\\right)+G\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{a+b-5}{k-2}\\right),  | \n | E=\\frac{(a-1)(a-2)}{2},F=a(b-a-1),G=\\frac{1}{2}\\left[{(b-a)}^{2}+5a-3b\\right].  | \n\nLet\n\n | p(X)=\\sum _{k=0}^{a+b-3}{\\alpha }_{k}{X}^{k},  | \n\nthen we have\n\n | p(X)  | =E{(1+X)}^{a+b-3}+FX{(1+X)}^{a+b-4}+G{X}^{2}{(1+X)}^{a+b-5}  | \n |  | ={(1+X)}^{a+b-5}\\left[E{(1+X)}^{2}+FX(1+X)+G{X}^{2}\\right]={(1+X)}^{a+b-5}q(X),  | \n\nwhere\n\n | q(X)  | :=(E+F+G){X}^{2}+(2E+F)X+E  | \n |  | ={\\displaystyle \\frac{(b-1)(b-2)}{2}}{X}^{2}+(ab-4a+2)X+{\\displaystyle \\frac{(a-1)(a-2)}{2}}  | \n\nSince {(1+X)}^{a+b-5} has clearly real roots, to apply Theorem 6.8 we need to show that q(X) has only real roots. The discriminant \\mathrm{\\Delta } of q(X) is\n\n | \\mathrm{\\Delta }  | ={(ab-4a+2)}^{2}-4{\\displaystyle \\frac{(b-1)(b-2)}{2}}{\\displaystyle \\frac{(a-1)(a-2)}{2}}  | \n |  | ={(ab-4a+2)}^{2}-(ab-2a-b+2)(ab-a-2b+2).  | \n\nIf a/b\\le 3/5 then a+2b\\ge 4a+\\frac{a}{3} and 2a+b\\ge 4a-\\frac{a}{3}, so\n\n | \\mathrm{\\Delta }  | \\ge {(ab-4a+2)}^{2}-\\left(ab-4a+2+{\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{3}}\\right)\\left(ab-4a+2-{\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{3}}\\right)={\\displaystyle \\frac{{a}^{2}}{9}}\\ge 0.  | \n\n∎\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------